A state lies in the evaluation of the concatenation automaton on a word if and only if it is a left copy of a state reached by the first automaton on that word, or a right copy of a state reached by the second automaton on a suffix whose complementary prefix is accepted by the first automaton.

The proof inducts on the word from the right. On the empty word, evaluation is the epsilon-closure of the start states, and the closure law turns a left start state into the left clause and an epsilon crossing from an accepting start state into an empty accepted prefix; the converse rebuilds that crossing from an empty split. On a word extended by one letter, a left predecessor either stays in the first automaton or crosses by an epsilon edge once that letter reaches an accept state, while a right predecessor lengthens the suffix and cannot take an epsilon step. Conversely, a left clause steps the first automaton once more, and a right clause either crosses by an epsilon edge after an accepted full word, when the suffix is empty, or steps the second automaton once more along a nonempty suffix whose last letter agrees.
